INT. BARBERSHOP, SILVER GULCH - DAY

Striped pole. Brass spittoon. Through the window, a gallows is being quietly dismantled across the street.

JUDGE AMBROSE LITTLEFIELD, sixty, sits in the barber's chair under a white cloth. SHERIFF NATHANIEL WREN, forties, stands at the door with his hat in his hands. DOC SILAS FAIRWEATHER, a tall consumptive, sits by the stove. ROSALIND MARCHAND, called ROSIE, thirty, the barber, strops a razor. DEPUTY CALEB, nineteen, holds the broom.

LITTLEFIELD
Cut it short, Rosie.

ROSIE
Not too short, Judge. You'll feel the draught.

WREN
He won't be out of doors much.

LITTLEFIELD
Nathaniel, I'll be out of doors a good deal. Don't you worry.

DOC
You look better than you did in March.

LITTLEFIELD
I look like a man who sentenced the wrong brother.

WREN
You sentenced the one the jury found.

LITTLEFIELD
Juries find what you set in front of them.

ROSIE
Hold still.

LITTLEFIELD
Is the rope down yet?

CALEB
Yes, sir. Half an hour ago.

LITTLEFIELD
Good boy. Sweep the sawdust before the wind takes it.

WREN
The mare's saddled at the livery. Provisions are in the left bag.

LITTLEFIELD
You've been thoughtful.

WREN
Rosie packed the bread.

ROSIE
It's a day old.

LITTLEFIELD
Everything's a day old in this town.

DOC
Where will you go?

LITTLEFIELD
South. There's a Judge in Albuquerque who owes me a sherry.

ROSIE
You don't drink sherry.

LITTLEFIELD
No. But it's owed.

WREN
Ambrose.

LITTLEFIELD
Yes, Nathaniel.

WREN
I'll keep the bench warm.

LITTLEFIELD
You'll keep it clean. That's the harder thing.

Rosie wipes the razor on her apron and sets it on the shelf.

ROSIE
That's the last of it.

LITTLEFIELD
Thank you, Rosalind. What do I owe you?

ROSIE
Two bits.

LITTLEFIELD
Two bits for the cut of a lifetime.

ROSIE
Three, if you stay for the shave.

LITTLEFIELD
No. No, I believe I'll go as I am.
